      "assistant_text": "The index update encountered a formatting issue, but the synthesis page itself has been created successfully. Despite the index update failing, I can still answer your question and tell you about the synthesis:\n\n## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are expressions of the **same architectural principle**: centralizing critical system state at a single composition point during startup, preventing it from being discovered, mutated, or scattered across the codebase at runtime.\n\n**[[credential-scoping]]** centralizes credential injection: the Hub never holds the Anthropic API key in its own process environment. Instead, a secrets loader reads the key from configuration exactly once at startup and injects it only into the child process that needs it (Ingest or Query agent), immediately before that process starts. This prevents a compromised Hub from leaking credentials simply by inspecting its own environment.\n\n**[[runtime-paths]]** centralizes path resolution: the Hub resolves every file-system location in exactly one place at startup \u2014 a single path resolver reads configuration, validates everything exists, auto-creates missing directories, and reports effective values. No other part of the codebase is allowed to rediscover locations on demand.\n\nBoth patterns protect against the same failure modes:\n- **No ambient discovery**: neither credentials nor paths are left to be found in the wrong place at runtime.\n- **No scattered logic**: security and configuration are audited and evolved in one place, not duplicated across modules.\n- **Immutable after composition**: credentials are not inherited from parent processes and paths are not modified after initial validation.\n\nAdding a new credential or a new runtime location both follow the same pattern: extend the single composition point, not scatter changes across the codebase.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]] (the index update encountered a technical issue, but the synthesis page itself has been created).",
